The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}0&-1&-4\\-2&-3&-1\\-2&k&2\end{vmatrix}\\&=(0)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-3&-1\\k&2\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-1\\-2&2\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(-4)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-3\\-2&k\end{vmatrix}\\&=(0)\cdot ((-3)\cdot (2)-(-1)\cdot (k))\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (2)-(-1)\cdot (-2))\\&\quad +(-4)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (k)-(-3)\cdot (-2))\\&=(0)\cdot (k - 6)\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot (-6)\\&\quad +(-4)\cdot (- 2 k - 6)\\&=8 k + 18
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
8 k + 18&=0\\8 k&=-18\\k&=- \frac{9}{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=- \frac{9}{4}$.

The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}3&k&k\\3&3&0\\-2&-4&-3\end{vmatrix}\\&=(3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}3&0\\-4&-3\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(k)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}3&0\\-2&-3\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(k)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}3&3\\-2&-4\end{vmatrix}\\&=(3)\cdot ((3)\cdot (-3)-(0)\cdot (-4))\\&\quad -(k)\cdot ((3)\cdot (-3)-(0)\cdot (-2))\\&\quad +(k)\cdot ((3)\cdot (-4)-(3)\cdot (-2))\\&=(3)\cdot (-9)\\&\quad -(k)\cdot (-9)\\&\quad +(k)\cdot (-6)\\&=3 k - 27
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
3 k - 27&=0\\3 k&=27\\k&=9
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=9$.

The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}-1&-1&-3\\1&2&4\\k&-4&k\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-1)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}2&4\\-4&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}1&4\\k&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(-3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}1&2\\k&-4\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-1)\cdot ((2)\cdot (k)-(4)\cdot (-4))\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot ((1)\cdot (k)-(4)\cdot (k))\\&\quad +(-3)\cdot ((1)\cdot (-4)-(2)\cdot (k))\\&=(-1)\cdot (2 k + 16)\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot (- 3 k)\\&\quad +(-3)\cdot (- 2 k - 4)\\&=k - 4
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
k - 4&=0\\k&=4\\k&=4
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=4$.

The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}k&k&3\\-2&-3&-3\\-3&3&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&=(k)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-3&-3\\3&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(k)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-3\\-3&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-3\\-3&3\end{vmatrix}\\&=(k)\cdot ((-3)\cdot (-2)-(-3)\cdot (3))\\&\quad -(k)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (-2)-(-3)\cdot (-3))\\&\quad +(3)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (3)-(-3)\cdot (-3))\\&=(k)\cdot (15)\\&\quad -(k)\cdot (-5)\\&\quad +(3)\cdot (-15)\\&=20 k - 45
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
20 k - 45&=0\\20 k&=45\\k&=\frac{9}{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=\frac{9}{4}$.

The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}-3&k&-2\\-2&k&1\\4&0&-1\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}k&1\\0&-1\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(k)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&1\\4&-1\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(-2)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&k\\4&0\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-3)\cdot ((k)\cdot (-1)-(1)\cdot (0))\\&\quad -(k)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (-1)-(1)\cdot (4))\\&\quad +(-2)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (0)-(k)\cdot (4))\\&=(-3)\cdot (- k)\\&\quad -(k)\cdot (-2)\\&\quad +(-2)\cdot (- 4 k)\\&=13 k
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
13 k&=0\\13 k&=0\\k&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=0$.

