Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(3 x^{2} e^{y}\right)&=3 x^{2} y' e^{y} + 6 x e^{y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
6 x e^{y} + y' \left(3 x^{2} e^{y} + 2 y\right)&=3 x^{2}\\
\left(3 x^{2} e^{y} + 2 y\right)y^{\prime}&=3 x^{2} - 6 x e^{y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{3 x^{2} - 6 x e^{y}}{3 x^{2} e^{y} + 2 y}
$$

This formula applies where $3 x^{2} e^{y} + 2 y\ne0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(3 x^{3} \cos{\left(y \right)}\right)&=- 3 x^{3} y' \sin{\left(y \right)} + 9 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
9 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} + y' \left(- 3 x^{3} \sin{\left(y \right)} - 9 y^{2}\right)&=4 x\\
\left(- 3 x^{3} \sin{\left(y \right)} - 9 y^{2}\right)y^{\prime}&=- 9 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} + 4 x
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{- 9 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} + 4 x}{- 3 x^{3} \sin{\left(y \right)} - 9 y^{2}}
$$

This formula applies where $- 3 x^{3} \sin{\left(y \right)} - 9 y^{2}\ne0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(2 x \ln{\left(y \right)}\right)&=\frac{2 x y'}{y} + 2 \ln{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y' \left(\frac{2 x}{y} - 3\right) + 2 \ln{\left(y \right)}&=2 x\\
\left(\frac{2 x}{y} - 3\right)y^{\prime}&=2 x - 2 \ln{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{2 x - 2 \ln{\left(y \right)}}{\frac{2 x}{y} - 3}
$$

This formula applies where $\frac{2 x}{y} - 3\ne0$. This relation requires $y>0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(2 x^{2} \sin{\left(y \right)}\right)&=2 x^{2} y' \cos{\left(y \right)} + 4 x \sin{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
4 x \sin{\left(y \right)} + y' \left(2 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} + 3 y^{2}\right)&=9 x^{2}\\
\left(2 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} + 3 y^{2}\right)y^{\prime}&=9 x^{2} - 4 x \sin{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{9 x^{2} - 4 x \sin{\left(y \right)}}{2 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} + 3 y^{2}}
$$

This formula applies where $2 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} + 3 y^{2}\ne0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(3 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)}\right)&=- 3 x^{2} y' \sin{\left(y \right)} + 6 x \cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
6 x \cos{\left(y \right)} + y' \left(- 3 x^{2} \sin{\left(y \right)} - 3 y^{2}\right)&=9 x^{2}\\
\left(- 3 x^{2} \sin{\left(y \right)} - 3 y^{2}\right)y^{\prime}&=9 x^{2} - 6 x \cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{9 x^{2} - 6 x \cos{\left(y \right)}}{- 3 x^{2} \sin{\left(y \right)} - 3 y^{2}}
$$

This formula applies where $- 3 x^{2} \sin{\left(y \right)} - 3 y^{2}\ne0$.

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(y^{2} + y\right)&=\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(- 4 x - 2\right)\\
2 y\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}+1\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=-4
\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the terms containing $\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(2 y + 1\right)\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=-4\\
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=-\frac{4}{2 y + 1}
\end{aligned}
$$

This formula applies on real branches where $2 y + 1\ne 0$.

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
