:question/worked-solution:
A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with

$$
\begin{aligned}
-1&\le \sin\left(-4x\right)\le 1
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by $-4$, reversing both inequalities. Then add $1$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
4&\ge -4\sin\left(-4x\right)\ge -4 \\5&\ge -4\sin\left(-4x\right)+1\ge -3 \\-3&\le f(x)\le 5
\end{aligned}
$$

Both endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\in[-3,5]$.

A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with

$$
\begin{aligned}
-1&\le \sin\left(-3x+60^{\circ}\right)\le 1
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by $-3$, reversing both inequalities. Then subtract $4$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
3&\ge -3\sin\left(-3x+60^{\circ}\right)\ge -3 \\-1&\ge -3\sin\left(-3x+60^{\circ}\right)-4\ge -7 \\-7&\le f(x)\le -1
\end{aligned}
$$

Both endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\in[-7,-1]$.

A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with

$$
\begin{aligned}
-1&\le \sin\left(-2x\right)\le 1
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by $-3$, reversing both inequalities. Then add $3$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
3&\ge -3\sin\left(-2x\right)\ge -3 \\6&\ge -3\sin\left(-2x\right)+3\ge 0 \\0&\le f(x)\le 6
\end{aligned}
$$

Both endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\in[0,6]$.

A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with

$$
\begin{aligned}
-1&\le \cos\left(-4x+2\right)\le 1
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by $-2$, reversing both inequalities. Then add $2$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
2&\ge -2\cos\left(-4x+2\right)\ge -2 \\4&\ge -2\cos\left(-4x+2\right)+2\ge 0 \\0&\le f(x)\le 4
\end{aligned}
$$

Both endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\in[0,4]$.

A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with

$$
\begin{aligned}
-1&\le \cos\left(-4x+\pi\right)\le 1
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by $-4$, reversing both inequalities. Then subtract $1$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
4&\ge -4\cos\left(-4x+\pi\right)\ge -4 \\3&\ge -4\cos\left(-4x+\pi\right)-1\ge -5 \\-5&\le f(x)\le 3
\end{aligned}
$$

Both endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\in[-5,3]$.

